On desktop Linux the GPU process opens V4L2 devices. Out-of-process
video decode is ChromeOS-only, and video encode is in-process too.
FilePermissionsForGpu() only called AddV4L2GpuPermissions() for
ChromeOS and for the Chromecast allowlist. That helper allows
/dev/video-dec0..4, /dev/media-dec0..4, /dev/video-enc and
/dev/video-enc0..4. It does not allow /dev/videoN, so a camera node
stays out of the broker.

Call it for a desktop Linux build that compiled V4L2 in, then fall
through to AddStandardGpuPermissions() so the DRM render nodes are
still brokered. Chromecast already adds the V4L2 paths in the branch
above; the else-if avoids listing them twice. UseV4L2Codec() is false
when USE_V4L2_CODEC is unset, so a VA-API x86_64 build is unchanged.

--- a/content/common/gpu_pre_sandbox_hook_linux.cc
+++ b/content/common/gpu_pre_sandbox_hook_linux.cc
@@ -666,7 +666,13 @@
       AddChromecastArmGpuPermissions(&permissions);
       return permissions;
     }
+  } else if (UseV4L2Codec(options)) {
+    // Desktop Linux. The GPU process opens the codec nodes. The allowlist
+    // is the ChromeOS names (/dev/video-decN, /dev/media-decN,
+    // /dev/video-encN), which a udev rule points at the platform devices.
+    // Fall through so DRM render nodes are still brokered.
+    AddV4L2GpuPermissions(&permissions, options);
   }
 
   AddStandardGpuPermissions(&permissions);
   return permissions;
